# =============================================================================
# geometry/contactangle/channel_2D_H125um_eps0.86um_flat90.opts
#
# Same flat channel as channel_2D_H125um_eps0.86um.opts (Ly = 125 um = the
# wedge's midpoint width, eps = the wedge's eps), but the ice starts as a
# FLAT-SIDED BRIDGE -- a slab spanning the channel with vertical menisci, i.e.
# a 90-degree contact angle at t = 0.
#
# Why: the clipped-disc IC used everywhere else starts at
# arccos(-(Ly/2)/R) = 131.8 degrees. Relaxing from there to 60 is a 72-degree
# excursion, and most of the run is spent on it. Starting at 90 halves the
# excursion and gets to the plateau sooner.
#
# Built from the existing -bridge_* options rather than a new ic_type: the
# bridge is the intersection of two discs, so putting their centres far outside
# the domain makes the menisci nearly straight.
#   R = 50*Ly = 6.25e-3 m  ->  initial angle arccos((Ly/2)/R) = 89.43 deg
# The centres are deliberately CROSSED (cxL > cxR): disc L's left edge is the
# left meniscus and disc R's right edge is the right one, so their intersection
# is the slab. Putting them uncrossed gives an empty intersection and trips the
# solver's |cxR - cxL| < rL + rR check.
#
# Slab width 1.7249e-4 m is chosen so the ice area matches the clipped-disc runs
# (2.1561e-8 m^2) exactly, making the two IC families directly comparable.
# =============================================================================
-ic_type multi_grains
-dim 2
-Nx 618
-Ny 206
-Nz 1
-Lx 3.75e-4
-Ly 1.25e-4
-Lz 0

# No discs: the ice is the bridge below.
-bridge_cxL  6.351256e-03
-bridge_rL   6.250000e-03
-bridge_cxR -5.976256e-03
-bridge_rR   6.250000e-03
-bridge_cy   6.250000e-05

-eps 8.5840e-07
-eps_valid_temp -20.0
-delt_t 1.0e-4
-periodic 0
-wall_faces y0,y1
-dtmax 1.487e3
